# Cloudflare Pages redirects. With netlify.toml deleted, this file and _headers
# are the only host config in the repo.
#
# Format: <from> <to> <status>. First match wins, so order matters.
#
# Cloudflare Pages already strips .html and trailing slashes on its own, with a
# 308. The rules below pin the canonical form to a 301 rather than leaving it to
# a platform default that could change under us, and they are evaluated before
# the automatic stripping - verified against production. They are loop-safe:
# each target is an extensionless URL that serves a 200 and matches no rule here.

# Canonical URL form: the extensionless one, which is what canonical tags,
# og:url and sitemap.xml all declare. Listed one by one on purpose, exactly as
# A wildcard would also catch google7aa936a6fd430f8b.html, which must not move.
/index.html / 301
/portfolio.html /portfolio 301
/ai-automation.html /ai-automation 301
/ai-receptionist.html /ai-receptionist 301
/website-chatbot.html /website-chatbot 301
/whatsapp-chatbot.html /whatsapp-chatbot 301
/founder.html /founder 301
/co-founder.html /co-founder 301

# NOT redirected here, on purpose:
#
# google7aa936a6fd430f8b.html - the Search Console verification file, which is
# why no rule above matches it. Cloudflare Pages strips the .html unconditionally
# and a _redirects rule cannot stop it: Pages supports no 200-status rewrite,
# so there is no way to serve that path without a redirect. The 308 it issues
# stays on the same host and the token is served intact, which Search Console
# accepts, so verification holds. Moving that property to DNS TXT verification
# once the nameservers are on Cloudflare removes the dependency entirely.
#
# www.cerostudio.co -> cerostudio.co - Cloudflare Pages _redirects cannot match
# on hostname (domain-level redirects are unsupported), so this lives outside
# the repo as a Redirect Rule on the Cloudflare zone. It is already in place and
# returns a 301 to the apex; do not try to reproduce it here.
